          }
        ],
        "text": "The case concerns whether a probation\u2011conditioned search consent can be validly applied to Mark James Knights, who was convicted of a misdemeanor drug offense and placed on three years\u2019 probation, and whether California\u2019s practice of conditioning probation on consent to searches by any officer effectively enlists police officers in the probation program."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Government (Mr. Stewart); Government",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "d308d4d44a88da894367b357f5f3481ffc79da8b9be633d9c357a795835843bb",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-d308d4d44a88-0001-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 21,
              "start": 21
            }
          },
          {
            "document_hash": "d308d4d44a88da894367b357f5f3481ffc79da8b9be633d9c357a795835843bb",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-d308d4d44a88-0002-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 40,
              "start": 40
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The United States argues that any consent to search must bear a reasonable relationship to the respondent\u2019s probation status and points out that recidivism rates for misdemeanant probationers are substantially lower than for felony offenders."
      }
    ],
